 # Simplex Noise
 
-Also called: snoise, 3D Simplex Noise
+Also called: 3D Simplex Noise, Gradient Noise, Perlin Noise Implementation, Perlin Simplex Noise, Stefan Gustavson Simplex Noise, snoise
 
 ## Overview
-Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates continuous scalar fields by interpolating contributions from the corners of a simplex grid (tetrahedra in 3D) [S3, S4, unofficial]. It reduces directional artifacts inherent in cubic-grid noise by using a skewed triangular/tetrahedral lattice [S4, unofficial][S5]. It scales to higher dimensions with $O(n^2)$ complexity, compared to $O(n 2^n)$ for classic noise, and requires fewer multiplications [S6, S7, unofficial].
+Simplex Noise is a gradient-based procedural noise function that generates smooth, continuous scalar fields in 2D, 3D, and 4D space [S1, unofficial]. It solves the directional artifacts inherent in earlier grid-based noise functions (like Perlin Noise) by using a skewed simplex grid (triangles in 2D, tetrahedra in 3D) instead of a cubic grid, which reduces computational complexity and improves isotropy [S2, unofficial]. It is primarily used for terrain generation, texture synthesis, and as a density function $f(x,y,z)$ for implicit surfaces or voxel terrain [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A 3D coordinate vector $p = (x, y, z)$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
-**Outputs:** A scalar noise value $N(p)$, typically scaled to $[-1, 1]$ [S3, S4, unofficial].
+**Inputs:** A point $\mathbf{v} = (x, y, z)$ in 3D space.
+**Outputs:** A scalar noise value $N \in [-1, 1]$.
 
